<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<handout>
<title>Surfaces and Vectors</title>

<introduction>
<p>
Show all work and justify your answers. Explain the geometry whenever possible and include sketches where appropriate.

<url href="https://opentext.uleth.ca/apex-calculus/chapter_planar_curves.html"/>
</p></introduction>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<title>Recognizing Surfaces</title>

<p>
For each equation below:
</p>

<ol marker="a">
<li>Identify the surface.</li>
<li>Explain how you recognized it.</li>
<li>Sketch the surface (use Geogebra 3D to help).</li>
</ol>

<p><m>x^2+y^2=9</m></p>

<p><m>\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\dfrac{y^2}{9}+\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1</m></p>

<p><m>z=9-x^2-y^2</m></p>

<p><m>x^2-y^2-z^2=1</m></p>

<p><m>y=x^2-z^2</m></p>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<title>Intersections of Surfaces</title>

<p>
For each pair of surfaces:
</p>

<ol marker="a">
<li>Find the intersection.</li>
<li>Describe the geometric object obtained.</li>
<li>State where it lies in space.</li>
<li>Sketch the surfaces and their intersection.</li>
</ol>

<p>
<m>z=x^2+y^2</m>
and
<m>x=0</m>
</p>

<p>
<m>x^2+y^2+z^2=9</m>
and
<m>z=x^2+y^2</m>
</p>



</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<title>Possible Intersections of Two Spheres</title>

<p>
Suppose two spheres have centers <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m>, and let <m>d</m> denote the distance between their centers.
</p>

<p>
List and describe all possible intersections of two spheres.
</p>

<ol marker="a">
<li>Describe each possible geometric intersection.</li>
<li>Draw a sketch for each case.</li>
<li>State the relationship between the radii and the distance <m>d</m>.</li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<title>Vector Operations</title>

<p>
Let
<m>
\mathbf{u}=\langle 2,-1,3\rangle,
\qquad
\mathbf{v}=\langle -4,5,1\rangle.
</m>
</p>

<ol marker="a">
<li>Compute <m>\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v}</m>.</li>

<li>Find <m>\|\mathbf{u}\|</m>.</li>

<li>Compute <m>\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}</m>.</li>

<li>Compute <m>\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v}</m>.</li>

<li>
Verify that the cross product is orthogonal to both vectors.
</li>

</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<title>Orthogonal Vectors</title>

<p>
Let
<m>
\mathbf{v}=\langle 2,-3,1\rangle.
</m>
</p>

<ol marker="a">
<li>
Find a nonzero vector <m>\mathbf{w}</m> such that
<m>
\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{w}=0.
</m>
</li>

<li>
Find a nonzero vector <m>\mathbf{u}</m> that is orthogonal to both
<m>\mathbf{v}</m> and <m>\mathbf{w}</m>.
</li>

<li>
Explain how you found <m>\mathbf{u}</m>.
</li>

<li>
What geometric relationship exists among
<m>\mathbf{u}</m>,
<m>\mathbf{v}</m>, and
<m>\mathbf{w}</m>?
</li>

</ol>

</exercise>

</handout>
